#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:50002
msgid "The instrument"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:50004
msgid "The preset `presets/coronagraph.lumos` places a star and a companion of contrast 10⁻⁴ at 6 λ/D behind a charge-2 vortex. We add a camera with read noise; at λ = 1.6 µm, D = 8 m and f = 100 m, λf/D = 20 µm, i.e. 4 pixels of 5 µm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:70002
msgid "Matched filter and ROC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:70004
#, python-brace-format
msgid "Each exposure gives a new noise realisation. The residual (frame - bias - star) is filtered by the template of the companion; without it, the statistic follows N(0, 1), with it N(d, 1), the deflection being $d = \\sqrt{\\sum s_i^2/\\sigma_i^2}$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:90002
msgid "Contrast curve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:90004
msgid "Without knowing where the companion is, the detection limit at each separation follows from the noise in an annulus of width λ/D of a residual image (Mawet et al. 2014). The contrast is relative to the peak of the off-axis PSF of the star, here the companion template scaled by its contrast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:110002
msgid "Detection capability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:110004
#, python-brace-format
msgid "Moving the companion over separations gives the deflection per unit contrast $k(r)$; the matched filter then detects a contrast $c$ with probability $Q(Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - c\\,k(r))$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/09_detection_capability.ipynb:130002
